          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-0e5866f7a24ddc1c\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The speaker directly and emphatically avows love as a settled, enduring emotional fact. “It’s final” gives the avowal limited commitment-like force, but he primarily presents his love as something already certain rather than undertaking a new obligation. His controlled, almost impatient response supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"“Final” may imply lasting relational commitment, but the phrasing is more explicitly constative—“I know”—than promissory or undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A direct spoken reply by the man to the woman’s repeated request for assurance that he loves her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs after conflict over what love demands and after the woman repeatedly presses for reassurance. His “Yes, I do” answers her question, while “I know it’s final” presents his love as certain and settled. His wish to stop further questioning makes the delivery restrained and impatient rather than reflex-like. Her subsequent “delight and doubt” shows that she receives the declaration as meaningful reassurance but remains uncertain about it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“But do you?” — “Yes, I do. I love you”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance directly answers a question about whether the speaker possesses loving feelings, strongly supporting truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“and I know it’s final. It is final”\",\"supports\":\"The speaker characterizes the love as certain and enduring; this reinforces T and gives slight support to a commitment-like relational finality.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“so why say any more about it” and later “accept it and have done”\",\"supports\":\"The controlled, closure-seeking delivery argues against treating the words as an affect-driven expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She receives the answer “in delight and doubt” and asks, “Are you sure?”\",\"supports\":\"The addressee treats the utterance as an assurance whose truth and certainty are at issue, supporting dominant T force.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the central avowal, with slight P support for the declaration’s asserted finality. No independent E force or residual core function requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
